WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.488
A plugin bundles skills, commands, hooks, subagents and MCP servers into one install.

00:00:06.838 --> 00:00:10.379
You get them from marketplaces, which are catalogs of plugins.

00:00:11.779 --> 00:00:16.857
Install one with /plugin install, its name, an @, and the marketplace.

00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:22.540
Its skills arrive with the plugin's name in front, like /superpowers:brainstorming.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.824
A plugin can be on for you alone,

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:28.693
or for everyone in the repository, through its committed settings.

00:00:30.093 --> 00:00:32.567
Four worth installing, each a different layer.

00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:37.489
Superpowers is skills: brainstorm, plan, test first, debug, verify.

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:41.173
This article was planned with its brainstorming skill.

00:00:41.523 --> 00:00:44.988
Playwright is MCP: a real browser the agent drives,

00:00:44.988 --> 00:00:49.609
like opening your dev server at phone width and taking a screenshot.

00:00:49.959 --> 00:00:54.586
Security guidance is hooks: it warns about risky patterns as files change,

00:00:54.586 --> 00:00:57.212
and reviews the diff when the agent stops.

00:00:57.562 --> 00:01:00.882
Claude Code setup is a skill that reads your repository

00:01:00.882 --> 00:01:04.986
and recommends automations for each layer, without changing a thing.

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:10.771
Run on this portfolio, it suggested a hook that enforces its Docker-only rule,

00:01:10.771 --> 00:01:13.976
and a /verify skill that runs all five checks.

00:01:15.376 --> 00:01:19.397
A plugin loads into every session and can run code on your machine,

00:01:19.397 --> 00:01:21.797
so read its hooks before you install it.

00:01:22.147 --> 00:01:26.307
Install only what you use: every skill description costs context.

00:01:26.657 --> 00:01:31.031
The signal to build your own: a second repository needs the same setup.

00:01:31.381 --> 00:01:34.581
Codex has plugins too, shared with ChatGPT.
